i think in my genre of the jewelry  business which is very interconnected  with hip-hop artists use jewelry to  express their individuality their  creativity instead of having a piece of  art hanging on the wall they prefer to  have it around their neck what's up gq  this is waffy from jewelry unlimited and  you're watching on the rocks  i started working in my dad's little  kiosk when i was 12. our average ticket  item was 50 so it was really from the  bottom up in 2008 i started my own  company and today is where we are  we have always operated as a family run  business where customers feel like  family i  enjoy selling jewelry face to face i  enjoy having relationships with clients  waffy responsible for at least about  four or five hundred thousand dollars  worth of the jury that i got shout out  to wifey jury unlimited i got this from  waffy these two was made by why are you  watching this you owe me a percentage  don't forget don't think i forgot this  qc piece was made for little baby p  who's the owner of qc wanted to do  something special for him last year it  has the qc logo but it also say can't be  with p liked it so much he's like make  me one too he's all emerald cuts in the  back invisible set the qc is raised with  like big stones pointers on the top the  cuban was also made to match this where  it alternates emeralds and round cut  diamonds it caused pee close to two  hundred thousand a piece sourcing of  diamonds it comes down to purchasing  power if there's a jeweler that doesn't  make that many pieces  they would buy diamonds just for that  particular piece if there's one who  is capable of purchasing larger  quantities than holding in stock get a  little bit better pricing and then there  are jewelers that are buying straight  from lines and cutting out the entire  wholesale market they get the best  prices and they buy the largest  quantities and i'm proud to say that i'm  dealing directly with the minds so this  is drummer gang logo this was made for  rapper out of la his name's greedo he's  currently incarcerated this whole  conversation and this vision was  communicated directly  by a phone while he's in a jail cell he  wanted to make this we did we wired us  the money and here it is and it's just  waiting for him to get out and be able  to enjoy it it's his logo  it's got  multicolored diamonds with this infinity  link with emeralds in the little  pockets wherever we could set them all  handset very heavy so something like  this would take 25 to 30 different parts  so this hand is one piece this glove is  a piece the drumstick is a different  piece because uh you can't make this out  of a single casting two hundred thousand  it's heavy i need to put it down this  one actually young thug placed the order  to make it for his friend i showed it to  him and he loved it so much he ended up  buying a different gift for his friend  and saying you know what just customize  it for me so we further customized it  where we added a second s on top i think  we are malleable in the way that we can  cater to the clients so if there was  something new that a client would bring  to me i would be very capable of  presenting it to them and executing the  idea might sound crazy from the  beginning but  i mean we've put full-on circuit boards  in  pieces of jewelry where like a fully  functional traffic light or a fully  functional rubik's cube everything is  crazy until it's not  this is another piece for little baby  again cbfw can't be with this one he  wanted to incorporate large stones and  this is pretty much the largest point as  you can put on a pennant like this these  are 70 pointers meaning each stone is  0.70 carat and sourcing these 43 i think  43 big stones in the air matching  quality it's a task so this is polo g's  1300 and then it has these street signs  that i uh intersect on the bale he  wanted the burgundy diamonds on top  three-dimensional this is a big piece  it's almost six inches so this polo g  this was i think when his first album  came out he wanted his album cover in  the face of the watch which was i've  never seen that before and this is a  paddock philly which was a lot less  money back then but now it's a lot more  but this is also apologies this is his  label oda only dreamers achieve this is  done in emerald cuts along with round  cuts around it which round is just your  basic round brailling cut these are the  other cut which is baguettes baguettes  tend to be skinnier longer stones  emerald cuts are shorter wider but they  take up less surface area and therefore  not only does it cost more per carrot  but it also you end up using more  carrots to make jewelry this is a piece  for somebody from the fpg camp fpg's  futures label we made him one and now  i've made like maybe two dozen of these  in different sizes that are for his crew  i made one like this for estg however  this is for one of his partners the  partner wanted it to be able to spin  yeah this one is about 60 000 maybe like  80 carats of diamonds this one was made  for qc artist bankroll freddy he's from  arkansas and he loves his state and  here's another one both of them for  bankroll freddie this particular one was  sixty thousand again used incorporating  emeralds everything is casted in its  entirety and the diamonds are all  handset bbs so  it gets expensive this one was 35 000.  these two were made for jarvante davis  this is his company gtd and this was a  piece he had me make earlier i'm not  sure why  this the pendant alone was 45 000. this  one the pendant alone was 25 000. so the  wolf pack piece was i made about three  years ago it's for the owner of wolf  pack global which is the label that  signed little baby it was also all  virgins had heavy heavy piece with these  gold accents about 75 000 for the  pendant the cuban was not included but  that was about 50 more so this this  pendant i made for one of our good  friends he's a local entrepreneur uh his  name is vic pennant includes larger  pointers on the bezel emerald cuts in  the back that's his logo in the middle  held up by this beautiful baguette  tennis chain that graduates from a  smaller to larger size and the larger  size really gets large it's about 12  millimeters in the middle very expensive  stones when you get into those sizes  yeah it's one of the prettiest pieces  watches is actually the reason i got in  the business i have a special affinity  liking towards watches these are a few  of the brands we carry rolex richard  mill audemars piguet paddock philly or  dominant brands today patek philippe is  my favorite brand i mean i've wanted one  since i was making 550 an hour and i i  was able to buy one  in um in 2008 which was probably the  best purchase i've ever made it was a 25  000 watch which is now worth 250 000  something very interesting is going on  in the watch market which is our economy  is seeing hyperinflation so  the inflation on luxury products or  through the roof for example this watch  right here pre-pandemic 120 000. today  300 000 is a great deal there's not many  investments one can make that they can  enjoy wear own  while making money and it goes across  the board even diamond pieces like these  this watch itself this is a patek  philippe 5726 which is a moon phase  larger case size  this used to be selling for  approximately ninety thousand today two  hundred thousand it's a good deal supply  again is so limited but anybody who  comes in to sell me a watch is not  leaving with that watch because i'm  buying it i attend these watch uh shows  i have my reputed dealers that i've been  working for years but sourcing them is  always sourcing them for the right price  is the is the challenge i mean  technically as a jeweler i need to  purchase the watch plane and then add  the diamonds to it now there's only a  few models i can say  that today cost less than  plane  and here's an example like this model  right here is the patek philippe 5980  this watch is probably the only watch  that would sell a little bit less than  the plane version would because of the  lack of production of this watch because  they're so hard to find and the demand  has skyrocketed same with these  skeletons the watch actually today the  plane watch has outperformed the iced  out watch i enjoy dressing it up when i  say dressing it up i mean icing it out  that in itself is an art because you get  to manipulate the diamond setting and do  things that have not been done before  these two are the exact same watch but  this one has been iced out with  significantly larger stones there's  about 60 different sizes of stones that  go into creating one of these this is  our big stone setting this is literally  exclusive just to us this is something  that is honeycomb flower set one of the  best settings available in the market  but this is just something with that  extra pizzazz where you were able to  manipulate 20 more carats of diamonds  into the same surface area that's  something that we did last year that has  been very very popular and for good  reason these are just  our presidential so rolex makes many  different models presidential being one  of the best investment pieces there's  solid gold there's rose gold white gold  yellow gold and there's a platinum right  here this is your classic i think a lot  of people associate a rolex with a solid  gold rolex with this type of bracelet  which is the presidential bracelet i  mean this is the 40th anniversary  presidential which comes with a green  face that again has skyrocketed in value  today trading at like 95 to 100 000 when  beginning of the pandemic i remember  selling some for 28 20 32  and now there's a hundred and  that's triple people have understood  that and it has made more money than the  stock market recently this is a special  piece this is our rm1103 red quartz it  is one of the lightest timepieces for  its size the entire case is fabricated  out of red quartz i think the retail on  this might be around 200 000 but trading  it around half a million here's a  a little bit older model it's a 1101  this one the case is made in rose and  titanium now trading at about 300 000.  this one is uh 1102 that came out after  the 1101 before the 1103 probably around  275 and this is a limited mancini i  think he's a race car driver that they  made in collaboration with titanium  around 260 000. and this this is a small  small sample of the pieces we've made we  as a company like to push the envelope  and try new things the biggest pieces  and the craziest pieces that i have in  mind are the ones i'm working on today  thank you gq for stopping by my shop  hopefully we can do another episode in  my new shop that opens up this year

